Description:
Fans of Roberto Bola&ntilde;o will enjoy The Night, the debut novel by Venezuelan author Rodrigo Blanco Calder&oacute;n. Amid nation-wide blackouts linked to Venezuela&rsquo;s ongoing political and economic crisis, the boundaries between reality and fiction begin to blur. Two writers and a psychiatrist tell varying accounts of a series of femicides. Join Calder&oacute;n and his translator Daniel Hahn to hear how they blend crime fiction and literary gameplaying in his prize-winning book.

	Rodrigo Blanco Calder&oacute;n is appearing remotely

	&nbsp;

Join bestselling authors Sin&eacute;ad Burke and Rosie Jones as they talk about their respective books, Break the Mould and Edie Eckhart and discuss how to empower young people to find confidence in their own identity, claiming their space in the world. Sin&eacute;ad Burke is an advocate, teacher and previously has been on the cover of Vogue &ndash; the first little person to ever do so. Rosie Jones is one of Britain&rsquo;s most popular stand-up comedians, regularly appearing on TV panel shows, and includes her cerebral palsy into her comedic style.

	&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

Orhan Pamuk, Nobel Prize-winning author of My Name is Red, Snow and Museum of Innocence, visits Edinburgh for a special Book Festival event to launch his masterful new novel Nights of Plague.
	&nbsp;
	A sweeping historical epic and a startling contemporary story, Nights of Plague shows Pamuk to be a writer at the top of his game. Translated from Turkish by Ekin Oklap, it&rsquo;s a blistering tale of murder, palace intrigue, fragile empire and wild incompetence as a plague ravages an imaginary island in the Eastern Mediterranean, against the backdrop of an ailing Ottoman Empire. Although it&#39;s set at the turn of the 20th century, this is a novel that&rsquo;s rife with suspense, and will resonate strikingly with readers&rsquo; own recent experiences of a pandemic. Join Pamuk as he reads from Nights of Plague and talks to writer and film-maker Bidisha about one of the most highly-anticipated novels of the year.

	Copies of the book will be available to buy at the venue on the night.

	Tickets to the event are &pound;16.00 [&pound;14.00 concessions) or you can choose to buy the book at the same time as your ticket for &pound;36.00 [&pound;34.00 concessions). Go to the How to Book page to see who qualifies for concession rates.


	&nbsp;

More than a million readers have awaited the sequel to Jessie Burton&rsquo;s bestselling debut novel. The House of Fortune revisits the address in Amsterdam where the events of The Miniaturist took place to meet Thea Brandt on her 18th birthday. Determined to enter adulthood on her own terms, Thea finds it difficult to elude the shadows of the past. Discover how the story continues and how Burton created her sumptuous 18th century world as she talks to journalist Alex Peake-Tomkinson.

	&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

When former punk and theatre director Andy Arnold took over the disused railway arches in Glasgow, he couldn&rsquo;t have known the legend it would become. From 1991 to 2015, The Arches was the beating heart of the city&rsquo;s cultural life. David Bratchpiece and Kirstin Innes&rsquo;s Brickwork is a biography of, and a love song to, this site of club nights, creativity and collaboration. Join them for an evening of song, comedy and stories chaired by music journalist Nicola Meighan.

	Following the discussion, in the multi-disciplinary spirit of The Arches, this event includes a short stand-up set by David Bratchpiece inspired by his years at the venue and an extract from Songs For A Scabby Queen, Kirstin Innes&rsquo;s live show from her novel, with the musician Outi Smith.

	&nbsp;

The UK&rsquo;s longest-running literary prizes are awarded in this prestigious event with broadcaster Sally Magnusson, who hosts a celebration of the year&rsquo;s best works of fiction and biography. Join us as the Prize judges and University of Edinburgh students share their insights and the nominated authors discuss their books, before the live, much-anticipated announcement of this year&rsquo;s Prizes.
	&nbsp;
	On the 2022 fiction shortlist is Uschi Gatward with English Magic, Kaitlyn Greenidge with Libertie, Keith Ridgway with A Shock, and Bryan Washington with Memorial. Authors on the biography shortlist are Hanif Abdurraqib with A Little Devil in America: In Praise of Black Performance, Amit Chaudhuri with Finding the Raga: An Improvisation on Indian Music, Maria Stepanova, translated by Sasha Dugdale with In Memory of Memory: A Romance, and Frances Wilson with Burning Man: The Ascent of DH Lawrence.
	&nbsp;
	Who will join the illustrious list of winners of the James Tait Black Prizes, awarded annually by the University of Edinburgh? For further info on the Prizes go to: www.ed.ac.uk/events/james-tait-black

	Some authors are appearing remotely.

Discover the joy of expressing your feelings through art in this motivational and interactive workshop for children, based on illustrator and author Xavier Leopold&rsquo;s creative journal&nbsp;Art for the Heart. Xavier, along with motivational speaker Carl Konadu, helps you learn how to use art to become more self-aware, understand your feelings&nbsp;and build confidence.&nbsp;In this fun-filled event, you&rsquo;ll be inspired and motivated to love yourself and aim high in the future. Best of all, you&rsquo;ll get to express yourself through producing your own art from the heart.

	&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

Sabrina Mahfouz has often experienced the sly questioning underneath much of British society&rsquo;s treatment of her: as someone of Middle Eastern heritage, can she be trusted? Confronting what&rsquo;s at play here, These Bodies of Water takes us to the Middle Eastern coastlines which played a vital role in the Empire&rsquo;s stronghold and interweaves Mahfouz&rsquo;s own personal experiences. Today Mahfouz discusses her beautiful book of prose and poetry; history and memoir with Edinburgh Makar Hannah Lavery.

	Sabrina Mahfouz is appearing remotely.

	&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

Frustrated by literature events not being made accessible, disabled writers Julie Farrell and Ever Dundas conceived the Inklusion Guide, a useful resource for event organisers. This event celebrating the launch of their guide is chaired by Sin&eacute;ad Burke, disability activist and founder of Tilting the Lens. Farrell and Dundas come together with industry figures including Jenny Kumar (Literature Alliance Scotland) and Zaahida Nabagereka (Head of Social Impact at Penguin Random House UK). With a special performance reading from writer and poet Jeda Pearl.

	While it is not a legal requirement, we are mindful that clinically vulnerable people may be in attendance at this event and therefore encourage mask wearing. Masks will be available on the door.

	&nbsp;
